test-aborted-util.js: runtime blocker; util.aborted is implemented and four of the file's five cases pass. The fifth, "Aborted with gc cleanup", needs two things. Node holds the abort listener weakly against the resource, so collecting the resource retires it and a later abort leaves the promise pending; web-platform now provides that (addWeaklyHeldEventListener), and util.aborted uses it -- but only the canonical EventTarget can accept it, because the registration reaches that class's own internals. This lane installs no canonical abort globals, so the test's AbortController is the host's and the weak path is measurably not taken. The same case then inspects the promise, and util.inspect renders every Promise as {} because reading a promise's state needs a runtime helper -- NtsPromise is a struct the runtime owns, so this is compiler-lane work rather than an engine internal
